# Layers and rotation: Rotate on a box in flow, which takes up the rectangle that encloses it rotated.
Document (dev.stxt.pdf):
	Language: en
	Title: Layers and rotation · Rotation in flow
	Page:
		Paper: A4
		Margin: 20mm
	Styles:
		For style: chip
			Box:
				Padding: 6 10
				Background: tint
				Border: 1
				Border color: accent
			Typography:
				Bold: true
	Section:
		Text: Layers and rotation
			Style: topic
		Heading 1:
			Text: Rotation in flow
		Paragraph: `Rotate` turns a box clockwise by a number of degrees. A `Block`, `Text`, `Paragraph`, `Image` or `Barcode` in flow rotates about its center and takes up the rectangle that encloses it rotated, so the boxes around it move aside. Without `Width`, it measures its text on one line.
			Style: intro
		Text: Rotate: -90
			Style: label
		Text: Vertical label
			Style: chip
			Rotate: -90
		Paragraph: This paragraph starts below the whole rotated rectangle.
			Box:
				Margin: 8 0 18 0
		Text: Rotate: 90
			Style: label
		Text: Vertical label
			Style: chip
			Rotate: 90
		Paragraph: The text now reads from top to bottom.
			Box:
				Margin: 8 0 18 0
		Text: Rotate: 8, with Margin: 0 auto to center it
			Style: label
		Block:
			Style: chip
			Rotate: 8
			Box:
				Width: 220
				Margin: 10 auto 10 auto
			Paragraph: A block of 220 points, rotated 8 degrees with all it contains.
				Box:
					Margin: 0
		Paragraph: The next box starts below the tilted block, which takes up more height than it would unrotated.
			Box:
				Margin: 8 0 0 0
